---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import MiniBoard from '../../components/MiniBoard.astro';
import { findPuzzle } from '../../games/nonogram/puzzles';
import { EMPTY, FILLED, cluesFor, solveGrid } from '../../games/nonogram/solver';

const puzzle = findPuzzle('lighthouse')!;
const clues = cluesFor(puzzle.solution);

/* Board after the solver's first `steps` deductions. The stage boundaries
   below follow the solver's own order, so each picture is a real position. */
const boardAfter = (steps: number) =>
  solveGrid(clues, undefined, steps).grid.map((row) =>
    row.map((v) => (v === FILLED ? '#' : v === EMPTY ? 'x' : '?')).join(''));
const freshBetween = (a: string[], b: string[]) =>
  b.map((row, r) => [...row].map((ch, c) => (ch !== a[r][c] ? '*' : '.')).join(''));

const stageEnds = [0, 3, 6, 7, 9, 13, 15];
const boards = stageEnds.map(boardAfter);
if (!boards.at(-1)!.every((row) => !row.includes('?'))) {
  throw new Error('Lighthouse walkthrough no longer reaches a finished board; update stageEnds.');
}
const stage = (k: number) => ({ state: boards[k], fresh: freshBetween(boards[k - 1], boards[k]) });
---
<Base
  title="Walkthrough: solving The Lighthouse"
  description="A complete step-by-step solve of The Lighthouse, a 10 × 10 Island Sketch picture logic puzzle, with every deduction explained and shown on the board."
  section="guides"
>
  <p class="eyebrow">Island Sketch walkthrough · 10 × 10</p>
  <h1>Solving The Lighthouse</h1>
  <p class="lede">The Lighthouse is the first 10 × 10 sketch and a good one to learn the bigger board on: it uses the overlap rule, edge reasoning and finished-line crosses, and every one of its deductions follows from the last. Here it is from an empty grid to the finished picture, in six stages.</p>
  <p>If you’d like to try it first, <a href="/island-sketch/play?p=lighthouse">play The Lighthouse</a> and come back when you’re stuck. Teal squares in each diagram are the ones that stage adds.</p>

  <figure>
    <MiniBoard rows={clues.rows} cols={clues.cols} state={boards[0]} label="The empty Lighthouse board with its clues" />
    <figcaption>The starting board. Rows are numbered 1–10 from the top, columns 1–10 from the left.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Stage 1: the base of the tower</h2>
  <p>Look for the biggest clues compared with the line length. Row 10’s clue is <strong>10</strong>: a full line. Row 9’s <strong>8</strong> has slack 2, so the overlap rule gives its middle six squares (3–8). Row 8’s <strong>6</strong> has slack 4, which still leaves two certain squares in the middle (5 and 6).</p>
  <figure>
    <MiniBoard rows={clues.rows} cols={clues.cols} {...stage(1)} label="Rows 8 to 10 partly filled by overlap" />
    <figcaption>Three rows from the overlap rule alone.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Stage 2: the left edge</h2>
  <p>Now switch to columns, starting at the left where the edge helps. Column 1 is <strong>1</strong>, and row 10 has already filled its bottom square — so that’s the 1, and the other nine squares are crossed. Column 2 is <strong>2</strong> with its bottom square filled; a 2 that touches the bottom edge must be rows 9–10, so the rest is crossed. Column 3 is <strong>3</strong> with rows 9 and 10 filled, so it must be rows 8–10.</p>
  <figure>
    <MiniBoard rows={clues.rows} cols={clues.cols} {...stage(2)} highlight={{ kind: 'col', index: 2 }} label="Columns 1 to 3 completed from the bottom edge" />
    <figcaption>Three columns finished by reasoning from the bottom edge.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Stage 3: the tower’s left wall</h2>
  <p>Column 4 is <strong>1 7</strong>. Its bottom two squares are filled, and the 7 is the last block, so the 7 runs up from the bottom: rows 4–10. The square above it, row 3, must be the gap between the 1 and the 7, so it’s crossed. The 1 is somewhere in rows 1–2 — we’ll come back for it.</p>
  <figure>
    <MiniBoard rows={clues.rows} cols={clues.cols} {...stage(3)} highlight={{ kind: 'col', index: 3 }} label="Column 4 filled from row 4 down" />
    <figcaption>Column 4: the 7 is pinned to the bottom edge.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Stage 4: the lamp and the windows</h2>
  <p>Columns 5 and 6 both read <strong>4 1 3</strong>, and both already have rows 8–10 filled. That run of three is the final 3, so row 7 above it is a gap. The 4 and the 1 have to fit in rows 1–6: that’s 4 + 1 + 1 = 6 squares in a space of six — a full line within a line. So rows 1–4 are filled, row 5 is the gap, and row 6 is the 1.</p>
  <figure>
    <MiniBoard rows={clues.rows} cols={clues.cols} {...stage(4)} highlight={{ kind: 'col', index: 4 }} label="Columns 5 and 6 completed" />
    <figcaption>The gaps at rows 5 and 7 are the lighthouse’s windows.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Stage 5: the right side mirrors the left</h2>
  <p>The picture is symmetrical, and so are the clues. Column 7 (<strong>1 7</strong>) works exactly like column 4; columns 8, 9 and 10 (<strong>3</strong>, <strong>2</strong>, <strong>1</strong>) work like columns 3, 2 and 1, from the bottom edge.</p>
  <figure>
    <MiniBoard rows={clues.rows} cols={clues.cols} {...stage(5)} highlight={{ kind: 'col', index: 6 }} label="Columns 7 to 10 completed" />
    <figcaption>Only rows 1 and 2 in columns 4 and 7 are still unknown.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Stage 6: the top</h2>
  <p>Back to rows. Row 1 is <strong>2</strong>, and columns 5 and 6 are already filled there — that’s the whole 2, so columns 4 and 7 are crossed. Row 2 is <strong>4</strong>: columns 1–3 and 8–10 are crossed, which leaves exactly four squares (4–7), so they’re all filled. That also places the 1 of columns 4 and 7 in row 2.</p>
  <figure>
    <MiniBoard rows={clues.rows} cols={clues.cols} {...stage(6)} highlight={{ kind: 'row', index: 1 }} label="The finished Lighthouse" />
    <figcaption>Finished: the lighthouse, with its lamp room at the top.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>What this puzzle teaches</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Start where clues are big.</strong> The three bottom rows gave the first foothold without any other information.</li>
    <li><strong>Edges are anchors.</strong> Every column was solved from the bottom edge: a block that touches an edge can only be in one place.</li>
    <li><strong>A clue can be a full line inside part of a line.</strong> In stage 4, once the 3 was placed, the rest of the clue exactly filled the space left.</li>
    <li><strong>Use symmetry as a check, not a shortcut.</strong> The right side mirrored the left because the clues did — always confirm with the clues rather than assuming the picture is symmetrical.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Ready for more? The <a href="/guides/nonogram-advanced">advanced guide</a> covers the techniques the 15 × 15 sketches need, or <a href="/island-sketch#puzzles">pick another sketch</a>.</p>
</Base>
